 "PMC8719689-r28", "PMC8719689-r29", "PMC8719689-r30", "PMC9472436-r1", "PMC9743658-r1", "PMC9743658-r4", "PMC9519127-r4", "PMC9519127-r9", "PMC9519127-r14", "PMC11103427-r1", "PMC11103427-r3", "PMC11103427-r5", "PMC12013119-r2", "PMC12346606-r1", "PMC12376923-r2", "PMC12697767-r2", "PMC12697767-r3", "PMC12697767-r6", "PMC12462602-r1", "PMC7174705-r1", "PMC7174705-r2", "PMC7174705-r3", "PMC13168333-r3", "PMC13484639-r1", "PMC13484639-r3", "PMC9974068-r2", "PMC9974068-r5"], "line_ids": [], "text": "{\"pmcids\": [\"PMC10458608\", \"PMC10522980\", \"PMC10145996\", \"PMC10295233\", \"PMC10386368\", \"PMC11846901\", \"PMC11615417\", \"PMC11117817\", \"PMC11057139\", \"PMC11480280\", \"PMC12333422\", \"PMC13104249\", \"PMC13057466\", \"PMC12890322\", \"PMC13298883\", \"PMC12907071\", \"PMC6316338\", \"PMC7918069\", \"PMC13529858\", \"PMC7673751\", \"PMC8268098\", \"PMC9011954\", \"PMC7248110\", \"PMC8705110\", \"PMC9025842\", \"PMC8169765\", \"PMC8719689\", \"PMC9472436\", \"PMC9743658\", \"PMC9519127\", \"PMC11103427\", \"PMC12013119\", \"PMC12346606\", \"PMC12376923\", \"PMC12697767\", \"PMC12462602\", \"PMC7174705\", \"PMC13168333\", \"PMC13484639\", \"PMC9974068\"], \"citations\": [\"row:PMC10458608-r4\", \"row:PMC10522980-r2\", \"row:PMC10145996-r1\", \"row:PMC10295233-r2\", \"row:PMC10386368-r9\", \"row:PMC11846901-r2\", \"row:PMC11615417-r3\", \"row:PMC11117817-r1\", \"row:PMC11057139-r1\", \"row:PMC11480280-r1\", \"row:PMC12333422-r1\", \"row:PMC13104249-r2\", \"row:PMC13057466-r3\", \"row:PMC12890322-r2\", \"row:PMC13298883-r3\", \"row:PMC12907071-r2\", \"row:PMC6316338-r1\", \"row:PMC7918069-r1\", \"row:PMC13529858-r1\", \"row:PMC7673751-r1\", \"row:PMC8268098-r1\", \"row:PMC9011954-r2\", \"row:PMC7248110-r1\", \"row:PMC8705110-r1\", \"row:PMC9025842-r3\", \"row:PMC8169765-r2\", \"row:PMC8719689-r1\", \"row:PMC9472436-r1\", \"row:PMC9743658-r1\", \"row:PMC9519127-r4\", \"row:PMC11103427-r1\", \"row:PMC12013119-r2\", \"row:PMC12346606-r1\", \"row:PMC12376923-r2\", \"row:PMC12697767-r2\", \"row:PMC12462602-r1\", \"row:PMC7174705-r1\", \"row:PMC13168333-r3\", \"row:PMC13484639-r1\", \"row:PMC9974068-r2\"]}", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "input_tokens": 38415, "output_tokens": 1280, "cost_usd": 0.27209125, "duration_ms": 11578, "answered_at": "2026-09-26T22:12:29+00:00"}
{"qid": "D-002", "kind": "D", "cell": "D", "route": "prose_fallback", "tokens_delivered": 7877, "prompt_sha256": "067141139c6bf13029cf2b7a2a1ab1fb30e35cf7a439389d71f276f3722b1be8", "row_ids": [], "line_ids": ["b7183438be8f0a20", "4c61c3a22d968cee", "517ac6f49b7835d4", "0c945b0a6564ff31", "a62134a1ff545f5e", "826fa423e730985a", "088a72fcbdb98f74", "e2294bedfb33c274", "abee10ba202efd96", "71b346cd6bfcc341", "9be504bda1bbbbdd", "5f7bb8fd7138b962", "7c73116a7b8da3c8", "662a55fa8401d717", "ad9a5775a7e9ddc4", "fc27342162a9e511", "4ebe26413fae1a46", "33602df042ce770b", "cb2974829b1e8af3", "e646a2143fb1ebca", "ac9a41750a3470db", "ef6f569f30184a40", "5d3fa4aad5a89e9e", "bd7bd866ac768e57", "585c39d8821da273", "c71237057c7f40a9", "5620290d10e5b9df", "82878fc9ea3b26e5", "b930a71a1006fde9", "65003e62868ae51e", "fde785b6e2c1cb68", "3334ce20991f880b", "cb693e23e652c116", "1cc480460e49d5db", "66c02bad8d6caf80", "4fc3ef2610a6cd36", "a5e832a49e1344a3", "9b169d902e42e356", "1cb6374b9f91c477", "99e8a04583ba994e"], "text": "{\"answer\": \"Divalent cations like Ca2+ are far more effective than monovalent ions (e.g., Na+) at compressing the electrical double layer and neutralizing the negative surface charge on plastic particles, which reduces electrostatic repulsion and lowers the energy barrier to aggregation; the critical coagulation concentration is inversely proportional to the sixth power of ionic charge, making divalent ions 50–83 times more influential than monovalent ions (PMC12376886:L46). Ca2+ produces stronger charge neutralization/screening than Na+ at the same ionic strength, causing zeta potentials of nanoplastics (and porous media) to become much less negative, promoting particle-particle and particle-surface attachment (PMC11899741:L77; PMC9142603:L85; PMC13213765:L44). Ca2+ can also form specific bridging interactions with surface functional groups such as carboxyl groups, further enhancing aggregation and retention beyond simple charge screening (PMC11899741:L77; PMC11543686:L77). In seawater, high natural concentrations of divalent cations (Ca2+, Mg2+) interacting with negatively charged carboxylated plastic surfaces are reported to drive large agglomerate formation (PMC7918069:L51).\", \"citations\": [\"PMC12376886:L46\", \"PMC11899741:L77\", \"PMC9142603:L85\", \"PMC13213765:L44\", \"PMC11543686:L77\", \"PMC7918069:L51\"]}", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "input_tokens": 11592, "output_tokens": 502, "cost_usd": 0.0849975, "duration_ms": 5960, "answered_at": "2026-09-26T22:12:29+00:00"}
{"qid": "D-001", "kind": "D", "cell": "D", "route": "prose_fallback", "tokens_delivered": 8298, "prompt_sha256": "63ba8feffc3e2bb4857e6ff10effdb4f069d6e80e0eda1aeaae448ffefa17613", "row_ids": [], "line_ids": ["3851218b18a7ab79", "9dc56a3dfeea1fe6", "6ade135fbe5140e1", "132d5dd3ad4742f1", "b53a3a7fdbff87cf", "5e363e95ce57b47c", "d9309b378c9eec16", "2b69b993093621d5", "76765b9624d862e8", "d5feafc8ac4885c4", "d7e7f39166014127", "f46915b70da5abf6", "c246df41b8b81164", "eaad155c92acec7d", "6d3f0986b6663b04", "147b5d753d670713", "e920bb9a1b2ae4b4", "e3d9a4583ae6486e", "4ad81066bfb71922", "a6674543501a66ff", "8b922d594ac88cfc", "7b277b15ec6811dc", "419bf377e53f5b7b", "7e0c0fd0df6cf062", "cdee14607562b47a", "3b8a60e9b64e85ba", "fb7743a34d2f5653", "58e489fadab9e05c", "4fcb7e08aa20c40a", "b73e9ff71766db12", "815489b82932ad85", "c4e91df81e96f752", "368406d62d3ff758", "197d6d3405180278", "174a0bfaec48ddd7", "ccc8c6c9a8e74147", "6b4ffb0763221074", "4e54a2bfaadb7796", "b308faeb86ea69c1", "9bde448b607f7594"], "text": "{\"answer\": \"An eco-corona/biofilm changes measured zeta potential because it deposits a new outer layer of biomolecules (proteins, humic/fulvic acids, amino acids, lipids, polysaccharides, EPS) whose own charged functional groups now dominate the electrokinetic slipping plane rather than the bare plastic surface. For example, humic/fulvic acids carry multiple carboxylic groups that impart negative charge, making eco-corona-coated microplastics more negative than uncoated ones (PMC10830523:L132, PMC10830523:L134). Similarly, adsorbed EPS or soil metabolites alter surface functional-group composition and charge distribution, and the effect depends on corona composition/molecular weight and the underlying particle charge (e.g., large charge reduction or even charge reversal for aminated PS-NH2 with high-MW EPS, while carboxylated/bare PS show little zeta shift), showing the corona's chemistry—not the plastic core—sets the measured charge (PMC13057466:L43, PMC13057466:L47, PMC13394419:L25, PMC13394419:L31). A thicker or more mobile corona layer can also change how charged groups are arranged/exposed at the surface, further modulating the measured potential and binding behavior (PMC10830523:L134, PMC4958399:L42).\", \"citations\": [\"PMC10830523:L132\", \"PMC10830523:L134\", \"PMC13057466:L43\", \"PMC13057466:L47\", \"PMC13394419:L25\", \"PMC13394419:L31\", \"PMC4958399:L42\"]}", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "input_tokens": 11702, "output_tokens": 534, "cost_usd": 0.08648499999999999, "duration_ms": 7090, "answered_at": "2026-09-26T22:12:30+00:00"}
